course: dictionary
lesson: 949
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Bloodsucker is one of them. In «Dracula stories made bats into scary bloodsuckers», it means a vampire from a story. In a science lesson, it means an animal that feeds on blood.
explain: Look at the words around the word. Is the sentence about animals and food, about spooky stories, or about people being unfair? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: When someone calls a person a bloodsucker, it is rude and informal. It means they take money from others or use them unfairly. Do not use it to hurt people, even if you hear other people say it.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence shows which one you need.
fact: A bloodsucker can be an animal that feeds on blood, such as a leech, a mosquito, a tick or a vampire bat.
fact: «Bloodsucker» can also mean a person who takes money from others or uses them unfairly, and this use is rude and informal.
fact: In stories, a bloodsucker is a vampire, but only three kinds of bat drink blood, and even they lap a few drops rather than suck.
fact: After a walk in long grass, check your skin for ticks, which are bloodsuckers too.
exercise: «Dracula stories made bats into scary bloodsuckers.» In this sentence, what does bloodsucker mean? | A bird that sings | A tick in long grass | *A vampire from a story | A greedy landlord
exercise: In a science lesson, bloodsuckers feed on the blood of other animals. What is a bloodsucker here? | A ghost | *An animal that sucks blood, such as a leech | A kind of plant | A person who takes money unfairly
exercise: Someone says, «He is a bloodsucker, the way he takes everyone's money.» What does it mean? | A vampire bat in a cave | A friendly neighbour who helps everyone | *A person who takes money from others unfairly | An animal in a science lesson
test: Which of these is a bloodsucker in the animal sense? | *A mosquito | A cloud | A bicycle | A sunflower
test: Why should you check for ticks after walks in long grass? | Ticks are good luck and bring you treats | Ticks only live in the sea | *Ticks can climb onto you and feed on your blood | Ticks are very sweet to eat
exam: Which sentence uses bloodsucker as a rude insult? | The vampire flew over the castle at midnight. | The bats in the cave ate insects all night. | *The loan shark was a bloodsucker, charging poor families huge fees. | A mosquito landed on my arm and bit me.
